TiO\(_2\)-ZnS Cascade Electron Transport Layer for Efficient Formamidinium Tin Iodide Perovskite Solar Cells
## ABSTRACT
Achieving high open circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) for tin-based perovskite solar cells is challenging. Here, we demonstrate that ZnS interfacial layer can improve the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and photovoltaic performance of formamidinium tin iodide \(\mathrm{(FASnI}_{3}\mathrm{)}\) perovskite solar cells. The TiO\(_2\)-ZnS electron transporting layer (ETL) with cascade conduction band structure can effectively reduce the interfacial charge recombination and facilitate electron transfer. Our best-performing \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cell using the cascaded TiO\(_2\)-ZnS ETL has achieved a PCE of \(5.27\%\) with a higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.380\,\mathrm{~V~}\), a short-circuit current density of \(23.09\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), and a fill factor of \(60.01\%\). The cascade structure is further validated with TiO\(_2\)-CdS ETL. Our results suggest a new approach for further improving the performance of tin-based perovskite solar cells
with a higher \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \).
## INTRODUCTION
Organic-inorganic hybrid halide perovskite materials have drawn enormous research interest in the past few years mostly due to their superior photovoltaic properties, such as extremely high optical absorption coefficients, tunable band gaps, high electron and hole mobilities and long carrier lifetimes.\(^{1-4}\) A certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \( 22.1\% \) has been achieved;\(^{5}\) the most efficient cells are however still based on methylammonium (MA) or formamidinium (FA) lead iodide as light absorbing material. The toxicity of Pb undoubtedly hinders the commercialization of this new technology, thus driving the scientific community to seek a less toxic replacement. The possible candidates enabling the perovskite structure formation are Cu, Ge, Sn, Sb and Bi,\(^{6,7}\) in which Sn-based perovskites are particularly good candidates since both Pb and Sn belong to group 14 of the periodic table and possess similar optical and electrical characteristics.\(^{8-10}\) Over the past three years a few groups have successfully fabricated lead-free Sn-based perovskite solar cells using both regular and inverted device structures.\(^{11-18}\) Since Sn-based perovskites have lower optical band gap (\( \mathrm{E_{g}} \)) than their Pb-based analogs, their devices thus generally produce higher short-circuit current densities (\( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \)), another factor that makes them attractive for solar cell applications.\(^{8}\) Yet the overall performance of Sn-based devices is still low, mainly hampered by the moderate fill factor (FF) (low film quality) and poor open circuit voltage (\( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \)) (interfacial barrier and imperfect device structure)
and poor reproducibility. Recently, Seok and coworkers reported efficient and stable formamidinium tin iodide \(\mathrm{(FASnI}_{3})\) perovskite solar cells in which they used a \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) –pyrazine complex to improve the perovskite film morphology and achieved a promising FF of \(63\%\) and a PCE of \(4.8\%\).<sup>19</sup> However, the reported \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.32\;\mathrm{V}\) was still relatively low considering the \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\) of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) is \(1.40\;\mathrm{eV}\). Similarly low \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) has also been reported in other Sn-based solar cells. In principle, the theoretical maximum of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) in solar cells is determined by the bandgap of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) after accounting for the voltage losses arising from radiative and non-radiative recombination processes. According to this scheme \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) should be approximately \(1\mathrm{V}\) but due to the tendency of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to self-dope by partial oxidation to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\), the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) in Sn-based perovskites is typically significantly lower. Therefore, solving the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) loss is a critical step in bringing the photovoltaic performance of Sn-based perovskite solar cells closer to their theoretical limit. Given that \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is defined by the energy difference between \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)'s conduction band minimum (CBM) and the perovskite's valence band maximum (VBM), one possible reason for the low \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) could be the shallow VBM of the Sn-based perovskites, which may limit the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) output.<sup>17</sup> Another possibility is that carrier recombination between electrons in \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and holes in Sn-perovskites may lower the quasi-Fermi level of the electrons. This process is more severe in the Sn-based perovskites than the Pb-based ones because Sn-perovskites possess higher background hole carrier densities, stemming from the facile oxidization of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\). Therefore, a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) improvement in Sn-based perovskite solar cells is expected if the recombination process can be suppressed. With these considerations in mind, we
set out to study a cascade ETL structure for \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells by growing ZnS onto \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). First, ZnS is an \(n\)-type semiconductor with a wide \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\) of \(3.5–3.8\ \mathrm{eV}\).<sup>20,21</sup> It has higher electron mobility than \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), potentially improving electron extraction and transfer. It has been reported to serve as an ETL for organic solar cells and interfacial passivation layer for quantum dot sensitized solar cells.<sup>20-23</sup> Second, the CBM of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) is closer to that of ZnS than \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), so cascading electrons move from \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) to ZnS and then \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). Third, the presence of the thin ZnS layer acts as an energy barrier inhibiting charge recombination between \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\). The relaxation of the photo-generated electrons is much faster than the carrier recombination at interfaces. Because the CBM of ZnS is higher in energy than the CBM of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), electrons relaxed to \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) will be inhibited to recombine with holes in \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) absorber at interface.
In this paper, we show that coating \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with ZnS is an effective approach to facilitate electron extraction from \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) into \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). As a result, the ZnS-coated \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) device outperforms the neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) one in \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) (380 vs. 270 mV) while maintaining comparable \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF, essentially yielding a PCE of 5.27%. Photoluminescence (PL), time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL), and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements confirm that devices employing the ZnS interface layer have a more efficient electron transfer pathway and lower interfacial recombination compared to those without ZnS. Our studies demonstrate that turning the ETL into an energy cascade structure is a viable approach to boost the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and efficiency of lead-free tin-based perovskite solar cells.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Device Fabrication
The process of preparing compact and mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers on FTO has been reported elsewhere.\(^{17,24}\) Firstly, a compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer was spin-coated onto FTO substrate (Tec15, Hartford Glass) from ethanol solution of titanium isopropoxide at 2000 rpm for 30 s, then annealed in air at \(500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 20 min. Subsequently, a mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer composed of \(20\,\textrm{nm}\) particles (Dyesol DSL 18NR-T) was spin-coated onto the compact layer using a diluted solution in anhydrous ethanol (1:3.5 weight ratio) at \(500~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(1\ \mathrm{min}\), then annealed in air at \(500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\). Finally, the annealed mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film was dipped into a \(0.02\;\mathrm{M}\) aqueous \(\mathrm{TiCl_{4}}\) solution at \(70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) and then annealed at \(500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\). The \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) and \(\mathrm{CdS}\) films were synthesized by using a SILAR method based on a method reported in literature;\(^{23}\) the mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film was alternately dipped into \(0.1\mathrm{~M~Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) or \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) solution in ethanol, \(0.1\mathrm{~M~Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) solution in methanol and deionized water with a volume ratio of 1:3. This SILAR process was repeated two times.The solvent-engineering method for preparing the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite film deposited on mesoporous layer is similar with the method reported in literature.\(^{25}\) The precursor solution, consisting of \(372.5\;\mathrm{mg}\) home-made \(\mathrm{SnI}_{2}\), \(172\;\mathrm{mg}\) FAI (Dyesol), and \(31.4\;\mathrm{mg}\) \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) (sigma, \(99\%\)) dissolved in \(723~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) N,N-dimethylformamide and \(81~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) dimethyl sulfoxide, was spin-coated on the mesoporous layer with a spin rate of \(3000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for 60 s. The \(\mathrm{SnI}_{2}\) synthesis was described in our previous work.\(^{17}\) \(300~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) diethyl ether was dropped on the spinning substrate during the spin coating process. The substrate was then annealed at \(70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) on a hotplate. For the one-step method, the
precursor and film deposition are similar except the use of diethyl ether anti-solvent. The solution of hole transporting material (HTM), consisting of \(32~\mathrm{mg}\) of Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) (Sigma Aldrich, \(99\%\) ) and \(3.6~\mathrm{mg}\) of 4-isopropyl-4'-methyldiphenyliodonium tetrakis(pentafluorophenyl)borate (TPFB) (TCI America) in \(1.6~\mathrm{mL}\) of chlorobenzene, was spin-coated on the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite film at \(1500~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~\mathrm{s}\) and then annealed at \(70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\). All these procedures were performed in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glovebox. To complete the device, a \(100~\mathrm{nm}\) thick Au electrode was thermally evaporated on top of the HTM layer. During the evaporation process, the sample was inevitably exposed to air for about \(10~\mathrm{min}\). The active area of the solar cells was \(0.15{-}0.18~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), defined by the overlapping area between the un-etched FTO glass and Au electrode.
## Film and Device Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films were performed on a CPS 120 INEL XRD with a \(\mathrm{Cu}\) Kα radiation source operating at \(40~\mathrm{kV}\) and \(20~\mathrm{mA}\). The morphology of the devices and films were characterized by a high-resolution field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Hitachi SU8030). Ultraviolet–visible (UV-vis) and absorbance spectra of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films were measured by a Shimadzu UV-3600 PC double-beam, double-monochromator spectrophotometer. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were characterized by a Thermo Scientific ESCALAB 250Xi system. External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum was characterized by an Oriel model QE-PV-SI instrument equipped with a NIST-certified Si diode. Photocurrent density-voltage \((J{-}V)\) curves
were characterized by a Keithley Model 2400 under AM1.5G simulated irradiation with a standard solar simulator (Abet Technologies). Light intensity of the solar simulator was calibrated by an NREL-certified monocrystalline silicon solar cell. EIS measurements are performed on a CHI electrochemical workstation. The energy of the valence band of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films was measured by using photoemission spectroscopy in air (PESA) (AC-2, Riken Keiki). The \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films were grown on mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and measured right after being exposed to air to minimize the oxidation of the films. Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) lifetimes were measured with a streak camera setup (Hamamatsu C4334 Streakscope). The instrument response function (IRF) was approximately \(2\%\) of the sweep window. A commercial direct diode-pumped \(100\ \mathrm{kHz}\) amplifier (Spirit 1040-4, Spectra Physics) produces a fundamental beam of \(1040\ \mathrm{nm}\) (350 fs, 4.5 W). This light was used to pump a non-collinear optical parametric amplifier (Spirit-NOPA, Spectra-Physics) which delivers high repetition rate pulses. The samples were excited with \(525\ \mathrm{nm}\), 0.1 nJ pulses.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The device structure of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells used in this study is shown in Figure 1a. Compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) were grown on FTO as the ETL. \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films were coated on the ETL layer. PTAA and evaporated gold film were used as HTM and back metal electrode, respectively. The energy band diagram is shown in Figure 1b, in which the \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\) of the tetragonal crystalline films of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) is 1.4
\(\mathrm{eV}^{8}\) and the VBM of \(\sim-4.7\ \mathrm{eV}\). These energies were estimated from the Tauc plot of the absorption spectrum (Figure S1) and photoemission spectroscopy in air (Figure S2), respectively. The CBM of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) film is estimated to be \(\sim-3.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) by subtracting \(\mathrm{E_{g}}\) from the VBM. Since the CBM of \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) (\(\sim-3.5\ \mathrm{eV}\))\(^{20,21}\) is between those of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (\(\sim-4.2\ \mathrm{eV}\)), photo-excited electrons in the CB of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) cascade into \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) via \(\mathrm{ZnS}\). Simultaneously, the energy barrier at the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZnS}\) interface would effectively block charge recombination, which should essentially increase the quasi-Fermi level for electrons and raise the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) value. We note that the CBM of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (\(\sim-4.2\ \mathrm{eV}\)) is much lower than that of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\), thus limiting the output \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\). Nevertheless, we certainly expect \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) enhancement by suppressing the recombination at the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) interface since \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) has a rather high background hole carrier density and thus the recombination would be significant if the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films have a direct contact with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). The VBM of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) is slightly higher than that of the PTAA, potentially pausing a barrier for hole carrier transporting. However, the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) film is briefly exposed to air during device fabrication given the nature of our current experimental setup and as a result the surface of the perovskite films is \(p\)-doped, producing an Ohmic contact with the PTAA layer.\(^{8,17}\) Therefore, even though an energy barrier exists between the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) and PTAA layers originally, hole carriers can still be effectively transferred.
We first studied film morphology by using SEM. Figures 2a and 2b show the comparison of neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) coated-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (denoted as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}-\mathrm{ZnS}\)) mesoporous films; \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}-\mathrm{ZnS}\) films were grown by using the successive ionic layer adsorption and
reaction (SILAR) method.\(^{23}\) The SEM images indicate that the addition of the ZnS layer does not alter the morphology of the underlying mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, suggesting the formation of a very thin ZnS layer. The presence of ZnS was independently confirmed by XPS. Ti 2p, O 1s, Zn 2p, and S 2p peaks were all observed as shown in the XPS spectra, Figure S3. The \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films were prepared by a solvent-engineering method, analogous to the one reported for the fabrication of efficient Pb- and Sn-based perovskite films,\(^{26-28}\) since the films made by the conventional one-step method showed poor surface coverage (Figure S4a).\(^{19}\) Note that all the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films used in this study include \(20\%\) \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\) as an additive to reduce the background hole carrier density and improve the solar cell performance.\(^{11,29}\) Figures 2c and 2d show the surface morphology of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films prepared by the solvent-engineering method on the neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS substrates, revealing uniform and high surface coverage for both substrates. Once again, coating \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with a very thin ZnS layer does not alter the film's overall morphology. During these experiments, we did not observe the phase separation on the surface of the perovskite films caused by excess \(\mathrm{SnF}_{2}\), a phenomenon that has been reported elsewhere.\(^{19}\) This is attributed to the use of diethyl ether as an anti-solvent in our fabrication procedure instead of the conventionally used chlorobenzene. In Figure S4b, it is obvious that the film prepared by an anti-solvent of chlorobenzene has worse film coverage. The uniform and pin hole-less perovskite films efficiently prevent the recombination caused by a direct contact between HTM and ETL, and thus allow us to systematically study the effect of the Zn interface layer
on the device performance. A cross-sectional SEM image of a completed \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cell employing mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS layer is shown in Figure 2e. A 500 nm-thick capping layer of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) is formed on top of a 700 nm-thick \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS layer to allow optimal light harvesting efficiency since Sn-based perovskites are known to have a lower absorption coefficient than Pb-based perovskites especially in the long wavelength range.
We further characterized our \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films using UV-vis spectroscopy and XRD. The optical absorption spectra of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS films are very similar, indicating that both film systems have similar composition and thickness. Moreover, the XRD patterns reveal an identical crystal growth habit for both substrates. As shown in Figure 3b, the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) films formed on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) both crystallize in orthorhombic structure (Amm2 space group) with no preferred orientation.\(^{14,19}\) These results indicate that the \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) interface layer does not affect the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) film quality, namely thickness and crystallinity, thus we can attribute the differences arising in the devices to the \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) layer alone.
Having established the quality of our films we then proceeded to the main focus of this work which was to investigate the effect of the \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) layer on the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cell performance. Figure 4a shows the \(J_{-}V\) curves of two representative \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cells without and with \(\mathrm{ZnS}\) layer. The neat mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) device achieved a PCE of \(3.69\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.290\ \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(23.14\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(54.90\%\) when measured under reverse voltage scanning (that is, from \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) to \(0\ \mathrm{V}\)). We observed a significant performance improvement in the \(\mathrm{ZnS}\)-coated \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) yielding a PCE of
4.72% with a \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) of 0.366 V, a \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of 22.94 mA cm\(^{-2}\), and a FF of 56.33% under the same measurement conditions. The photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Figure S5 and Table 1. It is clear that there is a significant enhancement in \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \), while the \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS and the neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) devices are comparable, implying that the ZnS layer does not hinder electron transfer from the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) to ZnS. The slightly better FF also supports our initial hypothesis of a more efficient electron transfer process and the consequent suppression of the recombination. Additionally, we found that the thickness of ZnS is critical to the device performance as demonstrated in Figure S6; the ZnS layer thickness was controlled by the number of the SILAR deposition cycles. In particular, when the ZnS is too thin, the \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) drops (but it is still better than that of the neat devices), probably because the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was not fully covered with the ZnS. On the other hand, when the ZnS becomes too thick, \( V_{\mathrm{oc}} \) improves further but at the same time \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) drops. The best device performance was obtained from samples with two ZnS deposition SILAR cycles.
To further study the advantages that the ZnS interface layer brings on the electron transfer, we compared PL and TRPL spectra of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films grown on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with and without ZnS. Figure 4b shows the PL spectra of the perovskite films on different substrates, both displaying an emission peak at about 870 nm. Note that the PL intensity of the film deposited on the mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS layer is much lower than that of the film deposited on neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrate. Since the two films exhibit very similar absorption bandwidth and peak maxima, the significantly reduced PL intensity can be attributed to the quenching effect (improved charge collection) caused by the
better electron transfer promoted by the ZnS. The TRPL results of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite films deposited on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with and without ZnS are shown in Figure 4c. The \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite film deposited on neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) has an estimated carrier lifetime of 2.52 ns. In contrast, the carrier lifetime of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite film deposited on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS is \(\sim2.5\) times shorter (\(\tau=0.85\) ns), implying a faster electron transfer process.
To further validate that the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS cascade structure can in fact reduce the interfacial charge recombination, we performed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements on the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells. Figure 4d shows the Nyquist plots of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells at \(0\,\mathrm{~V~}\) bias in dark. The corresponding equivalent circuit of the devices is shown in Figure S7a, including the series resistance \((R_{\mathrm{s}})\), transfer resistance \((R_{\mathrm{tr}})\), recombination resistance \((R_{\mathrm{rec}})\), and chemical capacitance \((C)\). \(^{30-32}R_{\mathrm{s}}\) can be estimated from the high-frequency intercept on the real axis. The \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) at low frequency and \(R_{\mathrm{tr}}\) at high frequency can be assigned to the recombination at the perovskite/ETL interface and the charge transfer at the HTM/perovskite interface, respectively. The Nyquist plots in Figure 4d show a main semicircle at low frequency. This main semicircle can be primarily attributed to the \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) and \(C\). We also measured the Nyquist plots of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cells under different bias voltage in dark. The corresponding fitted \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) values of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS-based cell are much higher than those of the neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) ones, Figure S7b. These results indicate that the solar cells using the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS cascade structure have a much lower interfacial recombination rate than the neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-based cells, as the
recombination rate is inversely proportional to \( R_{\mathrm{rec}} \). Therefore, the ZnS surface coating on mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) effectively inhibits the recombination of charge carriers and improves cell performance.
Figure 5a shows the best-performing \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cell built on mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS substrate, measured under reverse and forward voltage scanning. This solar cell achieved a PCE of \(5.27\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.380\ \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(23.09\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(60.01\%\) when measured under reverse voltage scanning and a PCE of \(4.90\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.373\ \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(22.98\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(57.14\%\) when measured under forward voltage scanning, showing a small hysteresis. Figure 5b shows the measured EQE spectrum of the solar cell using mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS, showing a high average value in the \(300{-}900\ \mathrm{nm}\) wavelength range. The integrated \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) calculated from the EQE curve is \(20.7\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) and is comparable to the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) obtained from the \(J_{-}V\) measurements. The small difference arises from the fact that the EQE curve was taken on a weaker light intensity compared to the \(J_{-}V\) curves which were measured under AM1.5G simulated irradiation. The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS-based device also achieved a steady-state efficiency of \(4.94\%\) with a steady-state current density of \(18.94\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) at a constant bias voltage of \(0.261\ \mathrm{V}\) (Figure 5c). Figure 5d shows the histograms of PCEs for \(20\ \mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells using mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS substrates. The average \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\), \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\), FF, and PCE are \(0.370 \pm 0.009\ \mathrm{V}\), \(22.35 \pm 0.69\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\), \(56.25 \pm 2.24\%\), and \(4.65 \pm 0.32\%\), respectively, proving a good reproducibility and high average performance of our \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cells. We also
fabricated these solar cells using the conventional one-step method\(^{14}\) for comparison (Figure S8). Devices prepared by the one-step method only achieved a PCE of \(2.47\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.262\;\mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(20.40\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(46.31\%\) when measured under reverse voltage scanning. The low performance can be mainly attributed to poor film coverage of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) absorber.
Finally, we examined another cascade-structured ETL by using cadmium sulfide (CdS) instead of ZnS. CdS has similar semiconductor properties with ZnS and has been used as an interface layer to promote electron transfer and reduce charge recombination in lead-based perovskite solar cells.\(^{33,34}\) The CdS films were also prepared by the SILAR method. Figure S9a shows the transmittance of the \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}\) substrate without and with CdS film. CdS with a relatively narrow band gap of around \(2.35\;\mathrm{eV}\) reduces the transmittance of the substrate in the wavelength range of 350-500 nm. Therefore, the CdS film reduces the absorption of \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite absorber, and is thus detrimental to the device's \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) output. Figure S9b shows the \(J_{-}V\) curves of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cell using mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with and without CdS. The \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cell using mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-CdS achieved a PCE of \(3.76\%\) with a higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.328\;\mathrm{V}\), a lower \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(22.02\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(52.11\%\) when measured under reverse voltage scanning. Similar to the ZnS film, the CdS one also improves the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cell, thus validating our general strategy for \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) improvement. From the toxicity point of view, ZnS is no doubt a better candidate than CdS, making it more suitable for incorporation in such solar cells. We should mention, however, that the
\(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and efficiency of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cells even with the improvement of the ZnS interface layers are still far lower than of Pb-based perovskite solar cells. Although the performance of tin based perovskite cells is significantly lower to date than those of the Pb based cells, the intrinsic properties of the tin perovskites and lead perovskites are comparable (e.g. band gap, details of electronic structure, etc) are similar. This suggests that once proper conditions are found for the optimal design of the solar cells the tin-based devices are expected to reach comparable performance efficiencies to the Pb based ones. In our device structure, we have to use mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) as an ETL scaffold, which is a barrier for improving the performance of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cells. Further \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and efficiency enhancements are expected from the replacement/elimination of mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and planar Sn-based perovskite devices.
## CONCLUSIONS
We have shown that coating mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with a thin ZnS layer to form a cascade structure can significantly improve the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and PCE of the \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) perovskite solar cells. The best-performing \(\mathrm{FASnI}_{3}\) solar cell built upon mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-ZnS scaffold achieved a PCE of \(5.27\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.380\;\mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(23.09\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), and a FF of \(60.01\%\) when measured under reverse voltage scanning, which is significantly better than the reference solar cell using neat \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). The enhancement in \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and subsequently PCE is mainly attributed to the ZnS interface layer effectively reducing the interfacial recombination while simultaneously facilitating the electron transfer, confirmed
by both optical (PL) and electrical (EIS) measurements. Our results demonstrate that optimization of the electron transport layer such as having a cascade structure will be essential in pushing the efficiency of Sn-based perovskite solar cells higher.
